  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hazel Woodwart (Hypoxylon fuscum) is a wood-decay fungus in the Hypoxylaceae family that forms distinctive fruiting bodies on dead hardwood, particularly hazel and other deciduous trees. This ascomycete produces dark, crusty stromata that are important decomposers in forest ecosystems throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dark Crusty Stromata",
      "description": "Forms hard, dark brown to black crusty fruiting bodies on wood surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Perithecia Arrangement",
      "description": "Contains numerous small flask-shaped perithecia embedded in the stroma",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Preference",
      "description": "Typically found on dead branches and logs of hazel, birch, and alder trees",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hypoxylon fuscum grows on dead and decaying hardwood in moist, shaded forest environments typical of New England woodlands.",
